---
.title = "ZML quickstart",
.description = "Build the MNIST example with Bazel and classify a handwritten digit on your CPU.",
.date = .date("2026-11-16T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
---
Start with a pretrained model. Bazel fetches the example's weights and test
images, then ZML compiles the model and predicts the digit in one image.

# [Before you start]($heading.id('before-you-start'))

- Use a Linux or macOS development machine with Git and internet access for the
  first build. See [Compatibility]($link.page('reference/compatibility')) for the
  framework's build targets.
- Install [Bazelisk](https://github.com/bazelbuild/bazelisk#installation) as
  `bazel` on your `PATH`. On macOS with Homebrew, run `brew install bazelisk`.
  Bazelisk reads `.bazelversion` from the ZML checkout and selects Bazel for you.

# [1. Get the ZML source]($heading.id('get-the-zml-source'))

```sh
git clone https://github.com/zml/zml.git
cd zml
git checkout --detach 8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636
```

The [selected revision](https://github.com/zml/zml/commit/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636)
selects Bazel 9.3.0 through `.bazelversion`. Run the remaining commands from
the checkout root.

# [2. Build and run MNIST]($heading.id('build-and-run-mnist'))

```sh
bazel build --@zml//platforms:cpu=true //examples/mnist
bazel run --@zml//platforms:cpu=true //examples/mnist
```

`bazel build` compiles the example. `bazel run` launches it with the weights and
test image data supplied by the target. The first build can take longer while
Bazel downloads dependencies and compiles them.

# [3. Read the prediction]($heading.id('read-the-prediction'))

The program prints a handwritten digit in the terminal, followed by
`RECOGNIZED DIGIT` and its prediction. Compare the predicted digit with the
image. The example chooses a random image each run.

To check the framework after a code change, run its test target separately.

```sh
bazel test //zml:test
```

# [Troubleshooting]($heading.id('troubleshooting'))

- **`bazel` command missing.** Check that the Bazelisk executable is on `PATH`.
  If you installed it as `bazelisk`, use that name in the commands.
- **Repository or dependency download fails.** Read the first Bazel error for
  the failing repository. Check access to that host, then retry the same target.

This procedure follows the upstream source and CLI. Execution on a recorded
host is pending.

